l or illicit drug use\X0A\\X0A\## Family History\X0A\\X0A\*   Non-contributory\X0A\\X0A\## Review of Systems\X0A\\X0A\*   **Constitutional:** Denies fever, chills, weight loss.\X0A\*   **Respiratory:** Reports progressive shortness of breath, dry cough, mild right-sided pleuritic chest discomfort.\X0A\*   **Cardiovascular:** Denies chest pain, palpitations, edema.\X0A\*   **Gastrointestinal:** Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain.\X0A\*   **Neurological:** Denies headache, dizziness, weakness, numbness.\X0A\\X0A\## Physical Examination\X0A\\X0A\*   **General:** Alert and oriented, appears mildly distressed due to shortness of breath.\X0A\*   **Vitals:** HR 110 bpm, BP 130/80 mmHg, SpO2 90% on room air, Temp 37.0°C.\X0A\*   **HEENT:** Normocephalic, atraumatic. Mucous membranes moist. No JVD.\X0A\*   **Lungs:** Decreased breath sounds at the right lung base. Dullness to percussion at the right lung base. No wheezes or crackles appreciated elsewhere.\X0A\*   **Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No peripheral edema.\X0A\*   **Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, non-distended. Bowel sounds present in all four quadrants.\X0A\*   **Neurological:** Grossly intact.\X0A\*   **Skin:** Warm, dry, no rashes or lesions.\X0A\\X0A\## Investigations\X0A\\X0A\*   **Chest X-ray:** Shows right-sided pleural effusion and associated atelectasis in the right lower lobe.\X0A\\X0A\## Assessment\X0A\\X0A\1.  **Right-sided Pleural Effusion with associated Atelectasis:** The patient's symptoms (progressive dyspnea, pleuritic chest pain), physical exam findings (decreased breath sounds, dullness to percussion at the right base), and chest X-ray findings are consistent with a right-sided pleural effusion causing compressive atelectasis. The etiology is unclear at this time but needs further investigation, especially given his age and smoking history, raising concern for malignancy.\X0A\2.  **Hypoxia:** SpO2 of 90% on room air, likely secondary to the pleural effusion and atelectasis.\X0A\3.  **Tachycardia:** HR of 110 bpm, likely compensatory for hypoxia and respiratory distress.\X0A\\X0A\## Plan\X0A\\X0A\1.  **Admit to hospital:** For further evaluation and management of the pleural effusion and associated symptoms.\X0A\2.  **Oxygen therapy:** Initiate supplemental oxygen to maintain SpO2 > 92%.\X0A\3.  **Thoracentesis:** Perform diagnostic thoracentesis to analyze the pleural fluid (cell count, protein, LDH, glucose, cytology, cultures) to determine the etiology of the effusion (transudative vs. exudative, infectious, malignant).\X0A\4.  **CT Chest with contrast:** Order a CT scan of the chest to better characterize the effusion, evaluate for underlying lung pathology (e.g., mass, consolidation), and assess for mediastinal involvement or lymphadenopathy.\X0A\5.  **Consult Pulmonology:** Obtain a pulmonology consultation for management of the pleural effusion and potential need for further procedures (e.g., pleural biopsy, chest tube placement).\X0A\6.  **Labs:** Order comprehensive metabolic panel, complete blood count, coagulation studies, and BNP.\X0A\7.  **ECG:** Obtain an ECG to rule out cardiac causes of dyspnea.\X0A\8.  **Monitor:** Closely monitor vital signs, respiratory status, and oxygen saturation.\X0A\